WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.056
Every time you open a page, a message

2
00:00:03.056 --> 00:00:05.180
races across the world and back.

3
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.568
Let's follow it.

4
00:00:07.518 --> 00:00:10.386
At home, your phone and laptop reach the

5
00:00:10.386 --> 00:00:13.254
router by Wi-Fi, and the desktop by cable.

6
00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:15.866
The router sends each request out

7
00:00:15.866 --> 00:00:18.116
to the internet, and the answer

8
00:00:18.116 --> 00:00:19.742
comes back the same way.

9
00:00:20.692 --> 00:00:22.924
A message from your laptop doesn't

10
00:00:22.924 --> 00:00:24.766
fly straight to the website.

11
00:00:25.146 --> 00:00:27.390
It hops from your home router to

12
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.790
your internet provider, then across

13
00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.920
the internet, router by router,

14
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.942
until it reaches the server.

15
00:00:34.892 --> 00:00:36.626
Your phone only knows the

16
00:00:36.626 --> 00:00:37.784
name school.example.

17
00:00:38.164 --> 00:00:40.306
It asks a DNS resolver, which

18
00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:41.758
asks the name servers.

19
00:00:42.138 --> 00:00:44.550
The address comes back, your phone

20
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.148
keeps it, and only then does it ask

21
00:00:47.148 --> 00:00:48.828
the website for the page.

22
00:00:49.778 --> 00:00:52.766
To send a message, each layer wraps it in

23
00:00:52.766 --> 00:00:55.568
its own header: the transport layer adds

24
00:00:55.568 --> 00:00:58.304
ports, the internet layer adds addresses,

25
00:00:58.484 --> 00:01:01.010
and the link layer adds the next hop.

26
00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.568
The receiver unwraps it layer by

27
00:01:03.568 --> 00:01:05.482
layer, back to the message.

28
00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:09.222
Before HTTPS sends anything private, your

29
00:01:09.222 --> 00:01:11.562
browser and the website shake hands.

30
00:01:11.942 --> 00:01:13.946
The site shows its certificate,

31
00:01:14.126 --> 00:01:16.376
the browser checks it, and both

32
00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:18.056
make the same secret key.

33
00:01:18.436 --> 00:01:21.118
From then on, everything travels locked.

34
00:01:22.068 --> 00:01:24.264
On a café's Wi-Fi, anyone nearby

35
00:01:24.264 --> 00:01:25.878
can capture your packets.

36
00:01:26.258 --> 00:01:28.274
With plain HTTP they can read

37
00:01:28.274 --> 00:01:30.338
everything, even your password.

38
00:01:30.718 --> 00:01:32.734
With HTTPS they still see the

39
00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:35.320
addresses and the port, but the page

40
00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.324
and the password are scrambled.

41
00:01:38.274 --> 00:01:40.680
Sensors in a mesh pass each reading

42
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.230
to a neighbour, hop by hop, until

43
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.736
it reaches the gateway.

44
00:01:45.116 --> 00:01:47.042
If a link fails, the mesh

45
00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:48.656
simply finds another way.

46
00:01:49.606 --> 00:01:52.078
A good AI agent asks before it acts.

47
00:01:52.458 --> 00:01:54.930
The teacher asks it to book the lab.

48
00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.286
The agent checks the details with her, and

49
00:01:58.286 --> 00:02:01.094
only after she says yes does it call the

50
00:02:01.094 --> 00:02:03.506
booking tool, then it reports back.

51
00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:07.336
Devices, routes, layers and locks: now

52
00:02:07.336 --> 00:02:09.580
you can see the internet at work.
